      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Supporters of segregation in the 1920s often invoked \"separate but equal\" doctrines, racial hierarchies, and eugenic theories to justify exclusionary policies. They frequently argued that race was immutable and that societal segregation inherently preserved cultural traditions while preventing social mixing. Additionally, many used religious, biological, and economic narratives to assert the superiority of white dominance and the necessity of keeping races apart for stability. Understanding these specific rationales will help you effectively counter their logic during your tournament debates.",
